      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Crown Copyright (c) 2009. Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Tako-tsubo cardiomyopathy is an increasingly recognised phenomenon characterised by chest pain, ECG abnormalities, cardiac biomarker elevation and transient left ventricular dysfunction without significant coronary artery obstruction."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIMS",
            "content": "To report the clinical and echocardiographic characteristics from a large single-centre Australian series of patients with Tako-tsubo syndrome."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We prospectively collected data on 23 consecutive patients presenting between November 2005 and November 2007. Baseline demographics, ECG, echocardiography and coronary angiography were performed on nearly all patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "All patients presented with chest pain; 87% were female. Various stressors were noted and cardiac Troponin-T was elevated in 91% of patients. All patients had non-obstructive coronary disease at angiography. 19/23 patients had initial and subsequent echocardiography. Mean ejection fraction was 50% at baseline and 64% at follow-up (p<0.0001). Right ventricular dysfunction was present in eight, dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction in two, diastolic dysfunction in seven and two patients had the mid-cavity variant."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "This large prospective single-centre Australian series of Tako-tsubo syndrome is in concert with previous published series. Complete recovery of left ventricular function on echocardiographic follow-up was typical. Although its pathogenesis remains unclear, early distinction from acute coronary syndromes is important and the prognosis is reassuringly good."
          }
        ]
      },
